      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the range of the function \\(f(x) = \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}\\), we start by substituting \\(a = \\arctan x\\) and \\(b = \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}\\). The function can then be rewrit:\n\n\\[\nf(x) = a + b\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the range of the expression \\(\\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}\\). Using the identity for the sum of arctangents:\n\n\\[\n\\arctan x + \\arctan y = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{x + y}{1 - xy} \\right)\n\\]\n\nApplying this identity to \\(x\\) and \\(y = \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{x + \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}}{1 - x \\cdot \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSimplifying within the argument:\n\n\\[\n\\arctan \\left( \\frac{1 + x + 1 - x}{1 + x - x + x^2} \\right)\n= \\arctan \\left( \\frac{2}{1 + x^2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nWe then see that \\(\\arctan \\left( \\frac{2}{1 + x^2} \\right)\\) is bounded by \\(-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) and \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) because:\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\frac{2}{1 + x^2} \\leq \\frac{\\pi}{4}\n\\]\n\nSo, we know:\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\arctan \\left( \\frac{2}{1 + x^2} \\right) \\leq \\frac{\\pi}{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the range of the function \\(f(x)\\) is exactly:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}}\n\\]",
