      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Since $\\frac{1-x}{1+x}$ is never negative or zero, and $\\arctan$ returns values in the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2})$, the domain of $f(x)$ is $(-1,1)$. From this knowledge, my hint is good, because  $\\frac{1-x}{1+x}$ isn’t negative or zero. You can show quickly that the domain is in this region (since $\\arctan$ returns values in the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2})$). Another thing that makes domain good is knowing that $\\tan$ does not exceed $1$ for $x$ in the range of $[-1,1]$. So \\[\\arctan(x) + \\arctan\\left( \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\right) = \\arctan \\left( \\frac{x + x - x^2}{x + x} \\right) = \\arctan (0) = 0\\] in the domain.",
